This article presents a global generalization of the classical Jacobi elliptic functions by developing the theory of an "authentic Jacobi group" within the realm of elliptic functions of two complex variables. Classically, Jacobi elliptic functions are defined on a two-dimensional torus with a specific triangular lattice structure; in this study, the classical group is expanded to a five-dimensional object grounded in the theory of five-dimensional elliptic functions. This approach extends the dimensionality of the original construction by leveraging the theory of double periodic functions. The elliptic five-folds associated with this group possess a five-dimensional lattice isomorphic to a five-dimensional face-centered cubic lattice. Furthermore, the transformation formulas of the Jacobi group are derived, along with an exploration of their symmetries, which reveal the {\it Dynkin diagram} symmetry of the classical group. Additionally, the paper examines the analytic properties of the authentic Jacobi group, its double sine kernel, and the five-dimensional Jacobi theta function. The work also establishes a multivariable extension of the classical Kummer congruence. The findings are illustrated through illustrative examples involving elliptic functions. This research represents an extension of the authors' prior study: \textit{The {\it double} sine kernel and its multivariable extension}, published in the Proceedings of the American Mathematical Society, Vol. 143, No. 10, 2015, pp. 4401-4412. The authors gratefully acknowledge Professor Hans-Ruedi Kuhn for his valuable suggestions regarding multivariate lattice sums. Finally, this work demonstrates an extension of the {\it elliptic five-fold kernel}, originally proposed by the second author in \textit{The kernel of the multivariate gamma function}, published in Proceedings of the American Mathematical Society, Vol. 143, No. 10, 2015, pp. 4387-4399. Specifically, the {\it five-dimensional kernel} kernel($z_1,\ldots,